**What it is** — The authors state that "speed-strength, strength-endurance and speed-endurance are not simply derivatives of strength, speed and endurance, but are totally independent qualities." Their evidence is that "an increase in absolute strength does not necessarily enhance any of these three qualities." So the combined qualities "should be placed alongside the latter in a group, each with its own particular means and methods of development (Verkhoshansky, 1963)" (PDF p. 268 / printed p. 257). The same paragraph frames force and velocity as qualities that, "at a high level of development are inversely proportional to one another (see Ch 4)," and says excessive development of both "is

